 {
  "coord": "263:0",
  "verdict": "FAIL",
  "category": "grammar",
  "note": "Sentence fragment: \"It was now so crowded...\" has no main clause linking to the previous one.",
  "replacement": "Pierre ran through various yards and side streets with his small burden and got back to the Gruzinski garden at the corner of the Povarskoy. At first he did not recognize the place he had set out from to look for the child. He could not, because it was now so crowded with people and with goods dragged out of the houses. Russian families had taken refuge there from the fire with their belongings, and among them were several French soldiers in assorted clothing. Pierre paid them no attention. He hurried to find the civil servant's family, so he could give the daughter back to her mother and go off to save someone else. Pierre felt he still had a great deal to do, and had to do it fast. He was flushed from the heat and the running, and at that moment he felt more strongly than ever the youth, vitality and resolve that had come over him when he ran to save the child. She had gone quiet now. She clung to Pierre's coat with her little hands and sat on his arm looking around her like a small wild animal. He glanced at her now and then with a faint smile. He thought he saw something touchingly innocent in that frightened, sickly little face."
 },
